    "response": "{\"decision\":\"established_flaw\",\"issues\":[{\"category\":\"unsupported_inference\",\"target_kind\":\"assertion\",\"claim_quote\":\"Participant experience functions as a generalized rupture in selfhood and social order across the packet.\",\"evidence\":[{\"excerpt_id\":\"EXC_5602e48df8631064\",\"quote\":\"There are some that I heard saying if you take omo and mix in a little water, let's say one big cup and stir the water, and drink the pregnancy will be terminated. It is the same with the tea leaves. You take the big packet you mix with water and then drink. Till the water turns, BLACK, yes. So it depends, they don't say it terminates in what months.\"}],\"whole_packet_no_support\":\"whole_packet_no_support\",\"mechanism\":\"The excerpt describes traditional practices related to terminating pregnancy, not a generalized rupture in selfhood and social order.\",\"material_consequence\":\"The claim makes an unsupported leap to a broader, existential consequence based on anecdotal evidence.\",\"counterconditions\":\"\"}],\"unresolved\":[],\"rationale\":\"The claim presents an unsupported inference about a profound societal impact based on a discussion of traditional remedies for pregnancy termination. The evidence does not provide a basis for this claim.\"}",
